  "learn": {
    "id": "L-002",
    "title": "Why fund assets can fall in a month when investors add money",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A fund's assets under management (AUM) change for two reasons: money coming in or going out (net flows), and changes in the market value of the investments the fund already holds.",
      "September showed both at work. Equity schemes took in a net ₹38,306.55 crore, yet AMFI's note says equity fund assets fell 4.3% to ₹37.54 lakh crore because of mark-to-market losses: the fall in the value of their holdings was larger than the new money.",
      "So a fall in assets does not by itself mean investors withdrew money, and a rise does not mean they added it. The monthly flow figures and the market's moves separate the two. An investor's own holding moves with the fund's NAV, which reflects the market value of its investments."
    ],
